<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haw wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1865–1890</span> · Also known as Haw rebellions</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Haw wars were raids and campaigns between about 1865 and 1890 by bands of Chinese irregulars, called Haw, who had fled southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a> and related rebellions. They ravaged Muang Phuan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> and other Lao and Tai lands. Siam's efforts to suppress them, and the sack of Luang Prabang in 1887, helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> extend its control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haw-wars.jpg" alt="Haw wars" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ผู้สร้างสรรค์ผลงาน/ส่งข้อมูลเก็บในคลังข้อมูลเสรีวิกิมีเดียคอมมอนส์ - เทวประภาส มากคล้าย · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Army_of_Thailand_in_Haw_wars_(1875).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
